  {
    "id": "ch4-002",
    "anchor": "the official Inquiry",
    "layer": "history",
    "note": "Under British shipping law, a serious accident at sea led to a formal investigation in court. A magistrate heard the evidence sitting with “nautical assessors,” experienced seamen who advised on matters of seamanship. Such inquiries were also held in British colonial ports.",
    "source": "Merchant Shipping Act 1894 (57 & 58 Vict. c. 60), Part VI, “Special Shipping Inquiries and Courts”, https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/Vict/57-58/60/part/VI/enacted",
    "status": "draft",
    "checker": null
  },
